  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nToday...\n\nLight rain and drizzle festered to the southeast of I-80 this \nmorning and early afternoon. Temperatures will remain in the low \n50s to mid 60s across the forecast area today. Overall rain \ncoverage will continue to gradually diminish, however areas of \ndreary drizzly conditions could linger into the evening and \novernight hours, as forecast soundings continue to indicate low \nlevel saturation.\n\nWednesday...\n\nDrier conditions are expected to return Wednesday, ahead of an \napproaching shortwave and surface cold front. Winds will become \ngusty out of the south Wednesday afternoon, reaching speeds of 20-\n35 mph. Highs tomorrow will rise back into the mid and upper 70s, as \nthe strong southerly flow draws in warmer air ahead of the front. \n\nModerate instability, up to 500-1500 J/kg is expected to pool across \nfar southeast Nebraska into western Iowa, ahead of the front \nWednesday. While overall deep layer shear will be lacking, up to 30-\n40 kts could sneak into locations along and east of the Missouri \nRiver, potentially supporting the development of a couple stronger \nthunderstorms, capable of producing hail and damaging winds. \nMachine learning probabilities support this hypothesis, \nhighlighting areas from south central Kansas through southern \nMinnesota Wednesday evening. \n\nThursday through Halloween...\n\nShowers and storms associated with the front should pass fairly \nquickly Wednesday night, with the majority of precip and\nstrongest remaining well to our north and east. Cooler \ntemperatures can be expected on Thursday, with highs topping out\nin the mid 60s and lower 70s. \n\nThe previously mentioned front will be pushed back northward into \nthe forecast area Friday and Saturday, as a shortwave trough begins \nto organize near the Texas panhandle. Expect increasing rain chances \nas showers overspread the region Friday and exit to the east with \nthe departing low, Saturday night. Highs on Friday will stay in the \nmid 60s to low 70s, and fall into the mid 60s across the region \nSaturday. Trick'R'Treaters and tailgaters will likely want to pack a \nponcho Halloween evening. While showers will be on their way out, \nmodels continue to trend slightly slower with this system's \ndeparture. \n\nSunday and Beyond...\n\nAn impressive negatively tilted low pressure system looks to take \naim on the central CONUS early next week. Luckily, temperatures are \nprogged to be at least a few degrees above normal, in the upper 50s \nto mid 60s, likely preventing any snow from reaching the surface. \nThis would likely be a vastly different outcome, if a system like \nthis were to impact the region a few weeks from now. Overall, NBM \nprobs give the forecast area a 50-65% chance of seeing at least an \ninch or more of additional rainfall Monday through Tuesday. However, \nthis system is still several days out, at the tail end of our \ncurrent forecast period. So stay tuned for additional updates and \nchanges as the details become more clear.",
